From dc9927e397ced43e4cbede13dc314cbbcde49fe8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petar Kirov <petar.p.kirov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 12:20:44 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] [SPIRV] Give nested conditions that share an exit merge
 blocks of their own

Early returns from nested conditions (`if (a || b) return x; if (c && d)
return y; return z;`) leave one block, the function's exit, as the
immediate post-dominator of every condition. The structurizer then gave the
nested headers merges that lay outside their parents' constructs, and
spirv-val rejected the module ("exits the selection ... but not via a
structured exit"). A first step now routes, innermost first, the edges from
a nested header's region into the post-dominator it shares with an
enclosing header through a new block, which becomes its merge; only a
region that reaches the exit through blocks it dominates is split, so the
step always makes progress. Four tests pinned the exit-dispatch variables
the old shape needed; regenerated, their output is the same program without
them, and still validates.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TrgPqCXtysTg54Ef5n1nm6
